CAYLEY-DICKSON-STYLE NORM LEDGER
==============================================================================
Each doubling pushes the first unprotected endpoints one dyadic level
deeper.  The visible gap halves, but endpoint debt doubles, so the
product unprotected*(gap/th) stays essentially fixed.

  d=2 ladder skip 14     gap/th=   1/33 unprot= 34 norm=34/33
  d=4 ladder skip 14     gap/th=   1/66 unprot= 68 norm=34/33
  d=8 ladder skip 14     gap/th=  1/132 unprot=140 norm=35/33
  d=16 ladder skip 14    gap/th=  1/264 unprot=280 norm=35/33

Interpretation:
  The 16-gate is not closing a cover; it is acting like a zero-divisor
  repair.  It kills the old unit witnesses and creates a kernel one
  dyadic level lower.  A proof should preserve this debt norm until an
  endpoint leaf appears.

